7,889 scored decisions, zero entries, ever. Measured against the real cost law
on a PERFECT book (infinite depth, one-tick spread, zero slippage), the
smallest sizeable target is 4.55% (0.3% stop) to 8.60% (3% stop), because the
1.5x reward-to-risk law applies to fee-inclusive terms: 1.5*(stop+fees)+fees.
Every setup targets the high of the last twenty 1-minute bars; the eight close
calls of today proposed 0.20% to 1.77%. Three to twenty-five times too small.

- minTargetDistance is fee-aware. Its job is "a swing high nearer than this can
  never size"; it computed that floor without fees (its own comment admitted
  it), so it never fired, and the resistance cap would cap a target down to a
  level that could never pay.
- derivedProjection: one 20-bar range above entry for the five setups that
  carried no projection. MICRO_BITE has had this shape since FIX-85. A quiet
  coin still fails at sizing, so it selects for coins that actually move.

Nothing relaxed: REWARD_RISK_MIN, bufferOf, REFERENCE_HAIRCUT, the stop, the
cost law and every gate are untouched; fee rate '0' reproduces the old
scenario byte for byte. judge/*.js re-pinned, audit §4.47.

